sentences.l:
And Hector, when he noticed his brother Polydorus
holding his entrails in his hands, bending down toward the ground,
down over his eyes was poured a haze;

and no longer then did he bring himself
for long to turn about far off, but opposite came to Achilles,
a sharp spear shaking, like flame;

He spoke, and having brandished it he sent forth his spear, and that Athena
with a breath turned back from glorious Achilles,
very softly having breathed;

but Achilles
eager rushed on to kill him, full of eagerness,
terribly crying out;

And Tros, Alastor’s son — he came opposite to his knees,
if in some way he would spare him, having taken him, and let him go alive,
and not kill him, having pitied one of equal age —

and he wounded Mulius, standing beside him,
with his spear in the ear; and straightway through the other ear
went the bronze point;

And Deucalion then, where the tendons hold together
the elbow, there through his hand he pierced him
with a bronze point;
